<Article
  title="DIFC vs ADJD vs Dubai Courts: an honest comparison"
  description="Three registries, three sets of rules and fees. Language, eligibility, witnesses, process and government fees compared in plain English."
  image="/images/article-difc.jpg"
  minutes={7}
>
  <p>
    A UAE will is registered with one of three government registries:
    <strong>DIFC</strong>, <strong>ADJD</strong> (the Abu Dhabi Judicial
    Department) or <strong>Dubai Courts</strong>. They all produce a valid
    registered will — but they differ in language, eligibility, witnesses,
    process and government fees.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here is the honest comparison, with the public government fees. No agenda:
    the right answer genuinely depends on your situation.
  </p>

  <h2>DIFC — the English-only option</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Language:</strong> English only, no translation required.</li>
    <li><strong>Who can use it:</strong> non-Muslims only.</li>
    <li><strong>Witnesses:</strong> 2, and they join the video appointment.</li>
    <li><strong>Process:</strong> online.</li>
    <li><strong>Government fees:</strong> ~10,000 AED (single), ~15,000 AED (mirror).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    DIFC sits inside Dubai's international financial centre and runs entirely
    in English — which removes the translation step. The trade-off is the
    highest government fee of the three.
  </p>

  <h2>ADJD — open to the widest audience</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Language:</strong> English + Arabic, with a certified translation.</li>
    <li><strong>Who can use it:</strong> Muslim and non-Muslim expats.</li>
    <li><strong>Witnesses:</strong> none.</li>
    <li><strong>Process:</strong> online, fully remote.</li>
    <li><strong>Government fees:</strong> 950 AED (single), 1,900 AED (mirror).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The Abu Dhabi Judicial Department registry has the lowest government fee
    and the widest eligibility. It requires a certified Arabic translation of
    your will — a step that needs a Ministry of Justice-registered translator.
  </p>

  <h2>Dubai Courts — the onshore middle path</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Language:</strong> Arabic, with a certified translation.</li>
    <li><strong>Who can use it:</strong> non-Muslims (declaration required).</li>
    <li><strong>Witnesses:</strong> none.</li>
    <li><strong>Process:</strong> online, fully remote.</li>
    <li><strong>Government fees:</strong> ~2,000 AED (single), ~4,000 AED (mirror).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What about timelines?</h2>
  <p>
    Government timelines depend on the registry and can change. Anyone who
    publishes a fixed number of weeks as a promise is guessing. The serious
    approach: choose the registry on fit first, then get a current, realistic
    expectation for that specific registry at the moment you register.
  </p>

  <h2>So which one should you choose?</h2>
  <p>Three questions settle most cases:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Where are your assets?</strong> Property, accounts, business interests — the map matters.</li>
    <li><strong>How fast do you need it?</strong> Each registry moves at its own pace.</li>
    <li><strong>What is your budget?</strong> Government fees range from 950 AED to ~15,000 AED.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This is exactly what a free Risk Profile Audit clarifies: 30 minutes, your
    situation mapped against the three registries, and a clear recommendation —
    with the exact fees for your case.
  </p>
</Article>
